  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/leporinus-friderici-2/",
      "html": "<p>Leporinus friderici is one of the longest-known Leporinus species. It was described in 1794 under the name Salmo friderici. It reaches up to 40 cm in length. Specimens bred in Indonesia, currently 5-7 cm long, are available.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/leporinus-friderici-2/",
      "html": "<p>The author of the original description, Marcus Eliser Bloch, wrote: \"This fish is a resident of Surinam's waters. I received it from the present governor, the Lord of Friderici, along with several other fish. He has an excellent meat, and belongs to the tasty fish of Suriname.\"</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/leporinus-friderici-2/",
      "html": "<p>In nature Leporinus feed mainly on fruits, seeds and insects.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/leporinus-friderici-2/",
      "html": "<p>In behaviour they are best compared with cichlids. They form a quite rough ranking among themselves, so specimens with completely undamaged fins are rarely seen. These injuries are harmless. If they feel threatened, they immediately join together into a dense shoal. They are best described as social fish that value the company of conspecifics, but do not necessarily treat them peacefully.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/leporinus-friderici-2/",
      "html": "<p>In the aquarium they reliably destroy all plants. They are ideal fish for large aquariums with silver dollars, large cichlids, catfish, etc.</p>"
    }
  ],
